Machine Learning Project Proposal
Monthly Precipitation Forecasting in Bishop, California

Project type: 
An applied machine learning approach to predictive weather forecast for a specific locality. A comparative study of a historical baseline, random forest regression, and small neural network, with ENSO indicators as potential predictors. 

The project applies supervised learning to a real-world environmental forecasting problem. It combines historical climate observations with machine learning methods to investigate whether future monthly precipitation can be predicted more accurately than with a simple historical reference. 

The scope is intentionally limited to Bishop, California, and monthly precipitation. The code and data pipeline will be organized so that another location could be added later, but multi-location generalization is not a core requirement.

Project Idea: 
Can machine learning models improve monthly precipitation predictions for Bishop, California, compared with a historical-average baseline, and does incorporating El Niño–Southern Oscillation (ENSO) indicators improve predictive performance. 

The main topic idea is to predict precipitation totals using historical precipitation, seasonal information, and ENSO climate indicators. This is special because ENSO is associated with broad changes in ocean and atmospheric conditions, but its local effects vary by region and season. This project tests whether ENSO information provides useful predictive signal for precipitation in a specific California location, rather than assuming that El Niño directly determines local weather.

The application also has a practical motivation: precipitation can affect outdoor recreation and climbing plans. Monthly forecasts would not determine whether a specific day or route is safe, but they may help characterize broader wet and
dry periods.

The primary research question is whether a machine learning model predict next-month precipitation at Bishop better than a historical average baseline. The secondary question is whether adding ENSO indicators improve predictive performance beyond calendar and recent precipitation features. 


These are the particular hypotheses that will be tested: 
Random forest regression may capture nonlinear relationships between recent precipitation, seasonality, and ENSO. 

A small neural network may learn useful patterns, but it may also overfit if the historical dataset is limited.

ENSO features may improve predictions, but the improvement may be small or inconsistent at a local scale.

The primary target will be total precipitation in the next calendar month. Unusually wet months will be analyzed as a secondary outcome after the historical distribution has been inspected. 

