Footnote on Data Feasibility: 
Need to inspect the length and completeness of a long-running station record near Bishop. A short or incomplete local record may not contain enough independent El Niño events to support strong conclusions. If needed, consider a suitable gridded precipitation product or a nearby official station, documenting the geographic trade-off

Proposed Methods / Pipeline

Data and Features:
Aggregate daily precipitation observations into monthly totals. Candidate predictors include calendar month, precipitation in the previous month, rolling precipitation totals over the previous three and six months, and ENSO index values that would actually have been available at forecast time.


Forecasting Setup:
Predict month t precipitation using information available by the end of month t−1. Avoid using centered climate indices or other features that inadvertently contain future information.



Models Compared: 
Historical-average baseline: Predict a month using the historical average for that calendar month, calculated from the training data only. This simple reference establishes whether more complex methods add value.
Random forest regression: Train an ensemble of decision trees on the same input features. Random forests can capture nonlinear relationships and feature interactions without requiring extensive feature scaling.
Small feedforward neural network (PyTorch): Train a compact network to predict a numerical monthly precipitation total. Use a validation period to choose settings, with a loss such as mean squared error and an optimizer such as Adam. Standardize inputs using training data only.



Workflow:
Collect precipitation observations and historical ENSO indices; record station, units, coverage, and provenance.
Clean observations, inspect missingness and station consistency, and aggregate daily precipitation into monthly totals.
Perform exploratory analysis: plot the time series, seasonal distributions, missing-data patterns, and precipitation extremes.
Construct lagged features and align them to the forecast date to avoid leakage.
Split observations chronologically into training, validation, and final test periods.
Fit the historical baseline and random forest; tune a small number of settings using training/validation data.
Train a small PyTorch neural network and monitor validation performance to reduce overfitting.
Evaluate all models on the same held-out test period; compare errors and inspect unusually wet months.
Run an ablation comparison with and without ENSO features to test whether ENSO adds predictive value.

Evaluation: 
Use mean absolute error (MAE) as a readily interpretable primary metric and root mean squared error (RMSE) to emphasize larger errors. For the secondary extreme-month analysis, define a wet-month threshold from training data, then report precision, recall, and/or a confusion matrix. Compare all models on the same test dates.

Interpretation: 
Compare observed and predicted precipitation over time, inspect the largest errors, and assess whether ENSO features help. Feature importance or feature-ablation results indicate predictive usefulness, not causation.

Limitations:
Precipitation can be intermittent and highly skewed, and extreme events are rare. The neural network may not outperform simpler models on a small dataset. The project will report the result honestly rather than assume that added complexity improves accuracy.







Planned Implementation:
The project will be implemented in Python. The initial version will prioritize a reproducible end-to-end pipeline over a complicated interface.

Libraries: 
Pandas and NumPy for data handling; Matplotlib for plots; scikit-learn for the baseline, random forest, preprocessing, and metrics; PyTorch for the neural network.

Code Components:

Data Ingestion: 
Load station data and ENSO indices; retain metadata and source notes.

Preprocessing: 
Validate dates and units, aggregate precipitation, and handle missing observations transparently.

Feature Engineering:
Create calendar and lagged precipitation features, then align ENSO indices without future leakage.

Baseline Model:
Calculate calendar-month averages using only training-period information.

Random Forest Model:
Train and evaluate a scikit-learn regressor.

Neural Network: 
Implement a small PyTorch regression model, training loop, validation, and prediction routine.

Evaluation and Visualization:
calculate metrics, plot observed versus predicted values, and save model-comparison tables.

