src/evaluation.py
results/figures/ and results/metrics.csv
requirements.txt
README.md

The idea is that the program structure will be modular enough that a different location could be added later by changing the station/data configuration, but multi-location testing outside the core scope of the project. 

Planned Demo - End Stage
The final demonstration will show a complete forecasting workflow, from historical data to a fair comparison of predictions.

Expected Demonstration Elements:

Historical Climate Visualization:
Monthly precipitation over time, with seasonal patterns and unusually wet months highlighted.

Model Comparison:
A table comparing the historical baseline, random forest, and neural network on the same held-out test period using MAE and RMSE.

Forecasting vs Observed Plot:
Visualize model predictions alongside actual precipitation for test months.

Extreme Month Analysis:
Show how well each approach identifies months above the selected wet-month threshold.

ENSO Ablation:
compare a model with ENSO features against one without them to see whether those indicators add predictive value.

Discussion:
Explain which approach performed best, where it failed, and what the results do and do not establish.

Expected Deliverables: 

Documented Python code, data-processing pipeline, saved evaluation metrics, figures, and a short report explaining methodology, results, limitations, and possible extensions.
The project is successful if it produces a fair, reproducible comparison and a defensible conclusion—even if the machine learning models do not outperform the baseline.

